<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibibio people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ibibio are a people of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, living mainly in Akwa Ibom State between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-river">Cross River</a> and the coast. Organised in village groups rather than large kingdoms, they are known for the Ekpo masking society and for wood carving, and they traded with the Aro and the coastal ports. They came under British rule as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of Southern Nigeria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibibio-people.jpg" alt="Ibibio people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alawode Olusegun · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibibio_dancers_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Ibibio, also known as the Ibom, are found mostly in Akwa Ibom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-river">Cross River</a> and the eastern part of Abia State. Their related groups are the Annang, Ekid, Oron and Ibeno, who share names, culture and traditions and speak closely related, more or less mutually intelligible varieties of Ibibio. The Ibibio themselves are divided into the Eastern Ibibio or Ibibio Proper, the Western Ibibio or Annang, the Northern Ibibio or Enyong, the Southern Ibibio or Eket and the Riverine Ibibio or Uruan.</p><p>Where they came from is not settled. They are reputed to be among the earliest peoples of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. Some scholars see a link with the central Benue Valley and the Jukun, and the most widely accepted account, drawn partly from oral testimony, traces the core Ibibio of the Afaha lineage to Usak Edet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>. The traditions tell of a first settlement at Uruan and of clashes with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> moving south, after which the Ibibio spread through their present lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Traditionally Ibibio society was made up of communities of large related families, each led by a constitutional and religious head, the Obong Ikpaisong. He ruled with the heads of the families, the Mbong Ekpuk, and the heads of the cults and societies, together forming a traditional council. The Ekpo society enforced his decisions: masked members acted as messengers of the spirits and as the community's military and police. Putting on an Ekpo mask meant surrendering earthly identity and assuming an ancestral one. Before the British began to abolish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the mid-1880s, society ranked people as freeborn, strangers or later immigrants, and slaves.</p><p>The Ibibio are known for woodcarving, raffia weaving and pottery, and many carvers of Ekpo masks live at Ikot Ekpene. Ibibio masks play with proportion, and contrast dark male masks with light female ones. Body art, including painting, hairdressing and modification, marks occasions such as marriage and childbirth, and the dot is regarded as the mark of beauty. Traditional religion centred on Abasi Enyong, the God of Heaven, Abasi Isong, the God of the Earth, and Abasi Ibom, the Supreme Being.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> existed the Ibibio governed themselves. They and the Annang, Oron and Eket were part of the Bight of Biafra before the British created Eastern Nigeria, and they became part of it under colonial rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> came through nineteenth-century missionaries, and Samuel Bill began work at Ibeno and founded the Qua Iboe Church. Akwa Ibom State was carved out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-river-state">Cross River State</a> on 23 September 1987, and a 1961 plebiscite divided the Ibibio and Annang between Nigeria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>. The first Ibibio Bible translation was presented at Uyo on 27 August 2020.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ibibio people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Aro emerged at Arochukwu in the late 17th century from wars and alliances among Igbo, <b>Ibibio</b> and Akpa groups, traditionally under leaders remembered as Nnachi and Akuma Nnubi.</blockquote><a href="/state/aro-confederacy">Aro Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It took over the delta trading states, the lands of the Igbo and <b>Ibibio</b>, and the remains of the Benin Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/protectorate-of-southern-nigeria">Protectorate of Southern Nigeria</a></li></ul></section>
